# Copy to .env and fill in what you need. .env is git-ignored and never
# reaches the browser: the API server keeps the key and hands the page a
# short-lived session token instead.
#
#   cp .env.example .env

# --- Optional: per-user storage (the real, multi-user setup) -----------------
# With these set, every person signs in (email + password) and their memories
# live in their own Supabase row, with photos in Supabase Storage — so data
# survives redeploys and nobody shares anybody else's memories.
#
# With them unset the app keeps everything in server/data/db.json exactly as
# it always did: one local store, no login — perfect for development.
#
# 1. Create a free project at https://supabase.com (no credit card).
# 2. Run server/supabase-schema.sql in the SQL editor.
# 3. Authentication → Providers → Email: leave "Confirm email" off, so
#    accounts can be created without an SMTP service.
# 4. Copy these from Settings → API:
# SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
# Bucket for photographs (created automatically on first boot):
# SUPABASE_PHOTOS_BUCKET=photos

# --- Required for the voice assistant ----------------------------------------
# From https://www.assemblyai.com/dashboard/api-keys
ASSEMBLYAI_API_KEY=

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Speak as an agent you already published (see `npm run agent:publish`).
# Unset, the app configures the companion inline for the session, which needs
# no public URL and is the quickest way to try it locally.
# AGENT_ID=

# Only needed to publish the companion as a stored agent. It is the public
# address AssemblyAI calls for the companion's tools, so it has to be reachable
# from the internet, e.g. an ngrok URL while developing.
# MEMORY_LANE_BASE_URL=

# Regional AssemblyAI host, if your account is pinned to one.
# AGENTS_API_BASE=https://agents.assemblyai.com/v1

# --- Optional: the companion's search engine --------------------------------
# The companion looks up what a word means, and searches the web when the
# question is about the world rather than about them. With none of these set
# it still works: meanings come from Wiktionary and searches from Wikipedia,
# so a fresh clone can answer "what is the capital of Japan".
#
# For real Google results, make a programmable search engine at
# https://programmablesearchengine.google.com/ and take its id as the cx:
# GOOGLE_SEARCH_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_SEARCH_CSE_ID=

# Or Brave's Web Search API, https://brave.com/search/api/:
# BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY=
